@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+---
+status: accepted
+date: 2026-11-10
+decision-makers: build worker (Claude), on a defect the owner confirmed 2026-11-10; second reader pending
+review-by: 2026-12-10
+---
+
+# Alpaca cancel-all: an order already pending cancel is canceled again
+
+## Context and Problem Statement
+
+ADR 0036 has cancel-all skip an order the node holds as `PENDING_CANCEL`, "since a second cancel risks a
+rejection". NautilusTrader marks an order `PENDING_CANCEL` when a cancel is sent, before any answer. A
+cancel that gets no answer, a read timeout above all (ADR 0039), leaves the client's handling with no event:
+the order stays `PENDING_CANCEL` in the node while it may still be working at Alpaca. A later cancel-all
+then skips exactly the order that most needs a cancel. The defect was confirmed and reproduced offline
+(`test_cancel_all_cancels_again_an_order_whose_cancel_had_no_answer`). Does cancel-all skip such an order?
+
+## Considered Options
+
+1. Keep the skip (ADR 0036, as Coinbase's adapter does).
+2. Cancel every open or in-flight order for the command's instrument and side, `PENDING_CANCEL` included.
+3. Skip it unless the client has recorded that its last cancel got no answer.
+
+## Decision Outcome
+
+Chosen: **option 2.** Supersedes in part ADR 0036's cancel-all: everything else there stands (the cache's
+open and in-flight orders, by ID, one at a time, each re-read at its turn and skipped if closed).
+
+A cancel with no answer is not proof the order is going away, so the node cannot know which of its
+`PENDING_CANCEL` orders still work; option 3 would need the client to track that and to clear it on every
+path that settles a cancel, and gains only the avoided round trip. The rejection ADR 0036 feared is
+already handled: a 422 on a cancel goes through the client's existing check, which asks Alpaca for the
+order and generates nothing if it shows `canceled` or `pending_cancel`, so a second cancel of an order
+Alpaca is already canceling yields no `OrderCancelRejected`. A 204 waits for Alpaca's `canceled` update, as
+any cancel does. Which of the two Alpaca answers is not documented on the page read for ADR 0036
+(UNVERIFIED). Upstream's Kraken spot, Binance spot, Bybit and Interactive Brokers adapters are said to take
+every open order for a cancel-all (UNVERIFIED this session; ADR 0036 read those files at the pin).
+
+### Consequences
+
+* Good: a cancel-all reaches every order of the instrument the node holds open, whatever happened to an
+  earlier cancel of it.
+* Bad: an order with a cancel still in flight gets a second cancel request, one more HTTP call.
+
+### Confirmation
+
+`scripts/canon lint` checks this record. `test_execution_client.py`: a cancel answered by a read timeout,
+then a cancel-all, sends two cancels and ends `OrderCanceled`; the existing refused-cancel tests cover the
+422 path. On paper, what Alpaca answers a cancel of a `pending_cancel` order is UNVERIFIED until a paper
+test sends one.
+
+## Evidence
+
+Read 2026-11-10. No network this session: nothing below was fetched for this record.
+
+* `packages/canon-alpaca/src/canon_alpaca/execution.py` in this checkout: `_cancel` logs a read timeout
+  and an undefined HTTP status with no event; `_cancel_refused` returns without an event when Alpaca shows
+  the order `canceled` or `pending_cancel` (read).
+* `docs/adr/0036-alpaca-adapter-order-management.md`: the skip, its reason, and its evidence lines for
+  `crates/adapters/{kraken,binance,bybit,interactive_brokers,coinbase}` at the pin (read; the upstream
+  files themselves UNVERIFIED this session).
+* https://docs.alpaca.markets/us/reference/deleteorderbyorderid-1.md — 204 or 422; nothing on a cancel of a
+  `pending_cancel` order (UNVERIFIED this session; as ADR 0036 records it).
+* `uv run --locked pytest packages/canon-alpaca`: the regression test fails with the skip and passes
+  without it; every other offline test passes (run).
